Still reproducible on the latest release (1.18.18), headless and interactive. Verified against `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` with an Ollama 0.32.9 backend. Captured request bodies via a stub at `baseURL`:

**1. Model-level `options` — silently dropped:**

```jsonc
// config: models["qwen3.5:9b"].options = { "reasoning_effort": "none" }
{"model":"qwen3.5:9b","max_tokens":32000,"top_p":1,"messages":[...]}
```

**2. `variants` — forwarded, but nested under `options`, which OpenAI-compatible backends ignore:**

```jsonc
// config: models["qwen3.5:9b"].variants.fast.options = { "reasoning_effort": "none" }
// (snake_case and camelCase behave the same)
{"model":"qwen3.5:9b","max_tokens":32000,"top_p":1,"options":{"reasoning_effort":"none"},"messages":[...]}
```

Ollama's `/v1` handler unmarshals into a struct with no `options` field, so this is silently discarded: the model still thinks (60 reasoning tokens, empty content with `max_tokens:60`). The same server honors a **top-level** `reasoning_effort:"none"` (17 tokens, instant answer).

**3. v2 config keys `settings` / `body` — not honored by 1.18.18 at all** (nothing appears in the request body).

Net result: there is no config-only way to inject a top-level provider field like `reasoning_effort` for `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` models, so users of Ollama thinking models (qwen3.5, gemma4, DeepSeek-R1 style) cannot disable reasoning without running a local proxy.

Expected: model `options` forwarded into the request, `variants`/`options` merged at the top level, or the documented v2 `settings`/`body` keys working in the openai-compatible adapter — any of these would fix it.

Related: #41294, #5674, #971.

## Update: implementation published 🎤

The voice MCP server is built and published to npm. Note: it's published under my own scope (`@yuvarjbhado`) since I don't own the `@opencode-ai` org on npm — the install snippet in the original post should use this name:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "voice": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@yuvarjbhado/voice-mcp"]
    }
  }
}
```

Or with OpenCode's `mcp` key:

```json
{
  "mcp": {
    "voice": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@yuvarjbhado/voice-mcp"]
    }
  }
}
```

What it provides:
- `voice_transcribe` — record + local Whisper transcription
- `voice_type` — record, transcribe, and type at cursor
- `voice_status` — check availability

100% local (faster-whisper), cross-platform (sox/ffmpeg), no cloud API. Source: https://github.com/YuvrajSinghBhadoria2/opencode-voice-mcp

Still happy to fold this into the opencode repo (docs or a `packages/` dir) if maintainers want it core — just let me know the preferred location.

Yes, `export` currently serializes only one Session and its messages, while `import` updates only that single Session row. As a result, exporting a parent Session does not include its subagent Sessions.


One behavior needs to be settled: should exporting the complete Session tree require a special flag, or should it be the default? I lean toward exporting the complete tree by default.


`export <session>` outputs JSON. Although the JSON is human-readable, I see it primarily as a portable Session archive, especially since it is currently the only supported way to move a Session between projects. Exporting only the root by default silently omits subagent transcripts, which conflicts with that purpose.


I also looked at how other Agent tools handle this. [Claude Code](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents) stores each subagent transcript as a separate JSONL file under `~/.claude/projects/{project}/{sessionId}/subagents/agent-{agentId}.jsonl`, while those records still belong to the same Session. [DeepSeek Harness](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/blob/master/.agents/notes/implemented/feature/2026-08-10-web-session-log-export.md) always passes `includeDescendants=true` for Web exports. Its ZIP contains the root log at `session.jsonl` and descendant logs at `subagents/<id>/session.jsonl`. Claude Code provides a useful reference for Session ownership, while DeepSeek Harness directly demonstrates complete-tree export. OpenCode can keep using JSON while including subagent Sessions in the same archive.

## Implementation

For the archive structure, I would prefer a flat list of Sessions:

```json
{
  "version": 1,
  "rootSessionID": "ses_root",
  "sessions": [
    {
      "info": {
        "id": "ses_root"
      },
      "messages": []
    },
    {
      "info": {
        "id": "ses_child",
        "parentID": "ses_root"
      },
      "messages": []
    }
  ]
}
```

`import` should migrate every Session, message, and part in the archive within a single transaction. If children already exist in the database and a root-only or legacy import would move only the parent Session, I would prefer rejecting the import to avoid splitting the existing Session tree.

### Compatibility

Changing the default output from a single-Session JSON object to a multi-Session envelope would be a breaking JSON schema change, and older importers would not be able to read the new files.

I think we could first add `--with-children` to include subagent Sessions, emit a warning to stderr when exporting a root Session that has children without the flag, and make the new `import` accept both formats. The complete tree could become the default later. Long term, I strongly believe exporting subagents should be the default.
